1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,151
Imagine you are about to sell your house.

2
00:00:03,151 --> 00:00:06,286
It is eighteen hundred square feet, on a quiet street in a small

3
00:00:06,286 --> 00:00:06,700
town.

4
00:00:07,050 --> 00:00:08,740
You ask two agents what it is worth.

5
00:00:09,090 --> 00:00:12,327
The first says two hundred sixty thousand dollars.

6
00:00:12,327 --> 00:00:14,810
The second says two hundred thousand.

7
00:00:15,160 --> 00:00:17,949
That is a sixty thousand dollar difference.

8
00:00:17,949 --> 00:00:19,240
Who do you believe?

9
00:00:20,240 --> 00:00:22,255
So you look at what actually sold.

10
00:00:22,255 --> 00:00:24,690
Five houses on your street, in the last year.

11
00:00:25,040 --> 00:00:28,210
A thousand square feet sold for one hundred forty thousand.

12
00:00:28,210 --> 00:00:30,570
Three thousand square feet sold for three hundred sixty

13
00:00:30,570 --> 00:00:32,780
thousand. The rest are in between.

14
00:00:33,130 --> 00:00:36,145
Put them on a picture. Size along the bottom,

15
00:00:36,145 --> 00:00:39,160
price up the side. Every dot is one house.

16
00:00:39,510 --> 00:00:41,360
Your eye sees it straight away.

17
00:00:41,360 --> 00:00:43,662
Bigger houses, higher prices.

18
00:00:43,662 --> 00:00:46,430
The dots lean upward, almost in a line.

19
00:00:46,780 --> 00:00:49,320
So grab a ruler and draw the line.

20
00:00:49,320 --> 00:00:51,860
Then read off eighteen hundred square feet.

21
00:00:52,210 --> 00:00:54,916
But hand the same ruler to three people, and you get three

22
00:00:54,916 --> 00:00:57,650
different lines, and three different prices.

23
00:00:58,450 --> 00:01:00,621
There has to be one best line.

24
00:01:00,621 --> 00:01:04,720
A line we can defend, with a number that says why it wins.

25
00:01:05,070 --> 00:01:07,584
Finding that line is called linear regression.

26
00:01:07,584 --> 00:01:10,510
By the end of this video, you will find it yourself,

27
00:01:10,510 --> 00:01:13,598
by hand, and the fair price will be two hundred thirty thousand

28
00:01:13,598 --> 00:01:14,200
dollars.

29
00:01:15,200 --> 00:01:16,744
Welcome to Math Lab TV.

30
00:01:16,744 --> 00:01:18,950
Where math is something you can see.

31
00:01:19,650 --> 00:01:21,760
Today you will learn how to score any line,

32
00:01:21,760 --> 00:01:23,870
by measuring how far it misses each dot.

33
00:01:24,220 --> 00:01:27,174
You will see the best line found, step by step,

34
00:01:27,174 --> 00:01:30,160
and what its slope and starting point really mean.

35
00:01:30,510 --> 00:01:33,113
And you will learn when to trust its predictions,

36
00:01:33,113 --> 00:01:35,500
and when a line is quietly lying to you.

37
00:01:36,100 --> 00:01:38,727
Drawing a line through dots sounds like a school exercise.

38
00:01:38,727 --> 00:01:41,910
It is actually one of the most used tools in the world.

39
00:01:42,260 --> 00:01:45,080
Whenever one number tends to move with another,

40
00:01:45,080 --> 00:01:46,600
people want a line between them.

41
00:01:46,950 --> 00:01:49,643
Price from size. Sales from advertising.

42
00:01:49,643 --> 00:01:51,891
Exam scores from hours of study.

43
00:01:51,891 --> 00:01:55,040
How long a program runs from how much data it gets.

44
00:01:55,390 --> 00:01:58,665
Every one of those lines answers the same kind of question.

45
00:01:58,665 --> 00:02:01,941
If this number goes up by one step, how much does that one tend

46
00:02:01,941 --> 00:02:02,520
to move?

47
00:02:03,120 --> 00:02:05,982
And it is the very first model in machine learning.

48
00:02:05,982 --> 00:02:09,472
The same idea, misses, a score, and a search for the smallest

49
00:02:09,472 --> 00:02:11,610
score, is how much bigger models learn.

50
00:02:12,410 --> 00:02:14,396
So let us build it from nothing.

51
00:02:14,396 --> 00:02:15,940
Back to our five houses.

52
00:02:16,290 --> 00:02:18,122
Here is a line. Any line.

53
00:02:18,122 --> 00:02:20,330
We need a way to say how good it is.

54
00:02:20,680 --> 00:02:22,358
The line is a price guesser.

55
00:02:22,358 --> 00:02:24,880
For every size, it points to a price.

56
00:02:25,230 --> 00:02:27,766
So for each house, we compare two prices.

57
00:02:27,766 --> 00:02:30,930
What the line guessed, and what the house really sold for.

58
00:02:31,280 --> 00:02:33,276
The gap between them is the miss.

59
00:02:33,276 --> 00:02:37,490
Statisticians call it the residual, the bit that is left over.

60
00:02:38,290 --> 00:02:41,687
Notice the direction. The miss is measured straight up and down.

61
00:02:41,687 --> 00:02:43,570
Not the shortest distance to the line.

62
00:02:43,920 --> 00:02:46,216
Why? Because up and down is price.

63
00:02:46,216 --> 00:02:50,600
We are guessing prices, so we measure our mistake in dollars.

64
00:02:50,950 --> 00:02:53,365
The size of each house is not in doubt.

65
00:02:53,365 --> 00:02:57,220
We know it exactly. Only the price is being guessed.

66
00:02:57,820 --> 00:03:00,580
There is a different method that uses the shortest,

67
00:03:00,580 --> 00:03:03,560
slanted distance, for when both numbers are uncertain.

68
00:03:03,560 --> 00:03:06,540
But that is a different tool, for a different job.

69
00:03:07,140 --> 00:03:09,649
Now draw the miss for every house.

70
00:03:09,649 --> 00:03:13,650
Some dots sit above the line, and their miss is positive.

71
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:16,810
Others are below the line, and their miss is negative.

72
00:03:17,160 --> 00:03:18,976
A good line should have small misses.

73
00:03:18,976 --> 00:03:21,390
So let us add them up into one score.

74
00:03:21,740 --> 00:03:23,451
Try the simplest line of all.

75
00:03:23,451 --> 00:03:27,300
A flat line at the average price, two hundred fifty thousand.

76
00:03:27,650 --> 00:03:31,400
In our mean versus median video, the average was the single

77
00:03:31,400 --> 00:03:33,826
number that best stood in for a whole group.

78
00:03:33,826 --> 00:03:35,590
Here it is, as a line.

79
00:03:35,940 --> 00:03:37,811
It ignores size completely.

80
00:03:37,811 --> 00:03:39,499
Every house gets the same guess.

81
00:03:39,499 --> 00:03:40,820
So it should score badly.

82
00:03:41,170 --> 00:03:43,754
Let us look at the misses for this flat line,

83
00:03:43,754 --> 00:03:47,412
one house at a time. The smallest house is a hundred and ten

84
00:03:47,412 --> 00:03:51,070
thousand below it. The biggest is a hundred and ten thousand

85
00:03:51,070 --> 00:03:51,720
above.

86
00:03:52,070 --> 00:03:56,071
Add up the misses. Minus one ten, minus thirty,

87
00:03:56,071 --> 00:03:58,878
zero, plus thirty, plus one ten.

88
00:03:58,878 --> 00:04:00,530
The total is zero.

89
00:04:01,530 --> 00:04:05,100
Zero. A perfect score, for a line that is plainly bad.

90
00:04:05,450 --> 00:04:07,671
The pluses and minuses cancelled out.

91
00:04:07,671 --> 00:04:10,255
The misses above wiped out the misses below.

92
00:04:10,255 --> 00:04:11,930
So a plain sum is useless.

93
00:04:12,930 --> 00:04:15,620
We need every miss to count against the line,

94
00:04:15,620 --> 00:04:17,281
whatever its direction.

95
00:04:17,281 --> 00:04:20,588
The fix is the one from our standard deviation video.

96
00:04:20,588 --> 00:04:21,220
Square them.

97
00:04:21,570 --> 00:04:25,232
Each orange miss becomes a square, with the miss as its side.

98
00:04:25,232 --> 00:04:27,170
Its area is the squared miss.

99
00:04:27,520 --> 00:04:30,570
A square is never negative, so nothing can cancel.

100
00:04:30,920 --> 00:04:32,641
And squaring does something else.

101
00:04:32,641 --> 00:04:35,300
It punishes big misses much more than small ones.

102
00:04:35,650 --> 00:04:38,167
One miss of twenty costs four hundred.

103
00:04:38,167 --> 00:04:41,604
Two misses of ten cost only two hundred together.

104
00:04:41,604 --> 00:04:45,040
So the best line hates being far from any one house.

105
00:04:45,840 --> 00:04:47,320
Add up all the squares.

106
00:04:47,320 --> 00:04:48,800
That total is our score.

107
00:04:48,800 --> 00:04:52,810
It is called the sum of squared errors, and smaller is better.

108
00:04:53,160 --> 00:04:55,820
For the flat line, the score is twenty six thousand.

109
00:04:56,620 --> 00:04:58,137
Now give the line some tilt.

110
00:04:58,137 --> 00:05:01,164
Here is a line through the cheapest and the most expensive

111
00:05:01,164 --> 00:05:01,600
house.

112
00:05:01,950 --> 00:05:04,820
It looks great. It passes right through three of the houses.

113
00:05:05,170 --> 00:05:06,997
Two misses of twenty five.

114
00:05:06,997 --> 00:05:09,962
Squared, that is six hundred twenty five each.

115
00:05:09,962 --> 00:05:11,560
The score is twelve fifty.

116
00:05:12,360 --> 00:05:14,344
Much better. But is it the best?

117
00:05:14,344 --> 00:05:17,680
Let us go too far the other way, a line that is too steep.

118
00:05:18,030 --> 00:05:21,438
Its score jumps to seven thousand two hundred fifty.

119
00:05:21,438 --> 00:05:23,840
Too steep is bad, too flat is bad.

120
00:05:24,440 --> 00:05:26,880
So let the line search for itself.

121
00:05:26,880 --> 00:05:31,147
It can do two things. It can pivot, to change its tilt.

122
00:05:31,147 --> 00:05:33,530
And it can slide, up and down.

123
00:05:33,880 --> 00:05:36,025
Watch the score as it pivots.

124
00:05:36,025 --> 00:05:39,160
The squares shrink, grow, and shrink again.

125
00:05:39,510 --> 00:05:42,620
Now it slides. Too high is worse, too low is worse.

126
00:05:42,970 --> 00:05:45,053
Think of the score as a valley.

127
00:05:45,053 --> 00:05:48,066
Every possible line is a spot on the ground,

128
00:05:48,066 --> 00:05:50,382
and the score is how high that spot is.

129
00:05:50,382 --> 00:05:52,930
We are looking for the lowest point.

130
00:05:53,530 --> 00:05:57,270
And at one spot, the score cannot get any smaller.

131
00:05:57,870 --> 00:06:01,414
The misses are minus ten, plus twenty, zero,

132
00:06:01,414 --> 00:06:03,408
minus twenty, and plus ten.

133
00:06:03,408 --> 00:06:06,510
Squared and added, the score is one thousand.

134
00:06:08,010 --> 00:06:11,332
That is it. The line with the smallest sum of squared errors is

135
00:06:11,332 --> 00:06:14,680
the least squares line, and finding it is linear regression.

136
00:06:16,180 --> 00:06:18,021
Be honest about one thing.

137
00:06:18,021 --> 00:06:21,501
Squaring is a choice. If we added plain distances instead,

138
00:06:21,501 --> 00:06:24,980
the end to end line would actually win, fifty to sixty.

139
00:06:25,330 --> 00:06:28,026
It is the same choice as mean versus median.

140
00:06:28,026 --> 00:06:31,623
Squared misses behave like the mean, and plain distances like

141
00:06:31,623 --> 00:06:35,664
the median. Squares are the standard, because they give one

142
00:06:35,664 --> 00:06:37,910
clean answer with a simple formula.

143
00:06:38,710 --> 00:06:40,984
We do not have to search by sliding, though.

144
00:06:40,984 --> 00:06:43,956
For a straight line, there is a formula that lands on the best

145
00:06:43,956 --> 00:06:44,910
line directly.

146
00:06:45,260 --> 00:06:47,511
Step one. Find the average house.

147
00:06:47,511 --> 00:06:49,944
The average size is two thousand square feet,

148
00:06:49,944 --> 00:06:52,980
and the average price is two hundred fifty thousand.

149
00:06:53,330 --> 00:06:56,684
Here is a lovely fact. The best line always passes right through

150
00:06:56,684 --> 00:06:59,670
the average house. So now we only need its tilt.

151
00:07:00,470 --> 00:07:04,219
Step two. For each house, measure how far it is from the

152
00:07:04,219 --> 00:07:06,950
average, both in size and in price.

153
00:07:07,300 --> 00:07:10,589
To keep the numbers small, size is counted in hundreds of square

154
00:07:10,589 --> 00:07:12,870
feet, and price in thousands of dollars.

155
00:07:13,220 --> 00:07:17,500
Step three. Multiply each pair together, and add them all up.

156
00:07:17,850 --> 00:07:21,175
Eleven hundred, plus one fifty, plus zero,

157
00:07:21,175 --> 00:07:23,870
plus one fifty, plus eleven hundred.

158
00:07:23,870 --> 00:07:25,730
That is two thousand five hundred.

159
00:07:26,080 --> 00:07:29,209
When a house is bigger than average and also pricier,

160
00:07:29,209 --> 00:07:30,786
the product is positive.

161
00:07:30,786 --> 00:07:32,170
That pulls the line upward.

162
00:07:32,520 --> 00:07:37,002
Houses that are smaller than average and also cheaper give a

163
00:07:37,002 --> 00:07:40,929
positive product too. Minus times minus is plus.

164
00:07:40,929 --> 00:07:44,855
Only a house that breaks the pattern, bigger but cheaper,

165
00:07:44,855 --> 00:07:46,320
would pull the line down.

166
00:07:46,920 --> 00:07:50,533
Step four. Square each size distance, and add them up.

167
00:07:50,533 --> 00:07:54,145
One hundred, plus twenty five, plus zero,

168
00:07:54,145 --> 00:07:57,001
plus twenty five, plus one hundred.

169
00:07:57,001 --> 00:07:59,100
That is two hundred fifty.

170
00:07:59,450 --> 00:08:03,042
That second number is just how spread out the sizes are,

171
00:08:03,042 --> 00:08:05,060
a close cousin of the variance.

172
00:08:05,410 --> 00:08:09,554
Step five. Divide. Two thousand five hundred over two hundred

173
00:08:09,554 --> 00:08:11,970
fifty is ten. That is the slope.

174
00:08:12,970 --> 00:08:16,766
Last step. Start at the average house and walk back to zero

175
00:08:16,766 --> 00:08:20,525
size. Two hundred fifty, minus ten times twenty,

176
00:08:20,525 --> 00:08:23,110
is fifty. That is the intercept.

177
00:08:24,110 --> 00:08:25,706
Check it on the picture.

178
00:08:25,706 --> 00:08:29,136
Start at the average house, two thousand square feet and two

179
00:08:29,136 --> 00:08:30,732
hundred fifty thousand.

180
00:08:30,732 --> 00:08:34,383
Walk back two thousand square feet, twenty steps of a hundred,

181
00:08:34,383 --> 00:08:36,667
and the price drops ten thousand each step.

182
00:08:36,667 --> 00:08:38,263
Two hundred thousand gone.

183
00:08:38,263 --> 00:08:39,400
Fifty is left.

184
00:08:40,200 --> 00:08:42,087
Here is the whole recipe as symbols.

185
00:08:42,087 --> 00:08:44,530
Each one is just a word we already met.

186
00:08:44,880 --> 00:08:47,084
x is size and y is price.

187
00:08:47,084 --> 00:08:50,642
x bar and y bar, with the little line on top,

188
00:08:50,642 --> 00:08:55,900
are the averages. And the big sigma means add them all up.

189
00:08:56,250 --> 00:08:59,760
b is the slope, and a is the intercept.

190
00:08:59,760 --> 00:09:03,550
Ten and fifty. Exactly the line our search found.

191
00:09:04,550 --> 00:09:08,667
So our line says, price equals fifty, plus ten times size,

192
00:09:08,667 --> 00:09:11,850
in thousands of dollars and hundreds of square feet.

193
00:09:12,200 --> 00:09:15,026
In plain dollars, that is fifty thousand dollars,

194
00:09:15,026 --> 00:09:17,830
plus one hundred dollars for every square foot.

195
00:09:18,180 --> 00:09:19,415
What does the slope mean?

196
00:09:19,415 --> 00:09:22,679
Ten means, every extra hundred square feet adds about ten

197
00:09:22,679 --> 00:09:24,320
thousand dollars to the price.

198
00:09:25,120 --> 00:09:27,315
It is a rate. Dollars per square foot.

199
00:09:27,315 --> 00:09:30,070
It is the single most useful number on the line.

200
00:09:30,420 --> 00:09:33,917
And the intercept? It is where the line crosses zero size.

201
00:09:33,917 --> 00:09:37,210
Fifty thousand dollars, for a house with no floor at all.

202
00:09:37,560 --> 00:09:38,866
That house does not exist.

203
00:09:38,866 --> 00:09:41,942
The intercept is often just a starting point that makes the line

204
00:09:41,942 --> 00:09:44,010
fit. Do not read too much into it.

205
00:09:44,810 --> 00:09:46,856
Now the question we started with.

206
00:09:46,856 --> 00:09:49,130
Your house is eighteen hundred square feet.

207
00:09:49,480 --> 00:09:51,571
Fifty, plus ten times eighteen.

208
00:09:51,571 --> 00:09:55,094
That is fifty plus one eighty, which is two hundred thirty

209
00:09:55,094 --> 00:09:56,140
thousand dollars.

210
00:09:57,640 --> 00:10:01,295
So the first agent was a bit high, and the second was too low.

211
00:10:01,295 --> 00:10:02,720
And now you can show why.

212
00:10:03,070 --> 00:10:06,160
Let us test the line on a house we already know.

213
00:10:06,160 --> 00:10:07,925
Twenty five hundred square feet.

214
00:10:07,925 --> 00:10:10,795
The line says fifty plus ten times twenty five,

215
00:10:10,795 --> 00:10:12,560
which is three hundred thousand.

216
00:10:12,910 --> 00:10:15,381
It really sold for two hundred eighty thousand.

217
00:10:15,381 --> 00:10:17,851
So that house sold twenty thousand below the line.

218
00:10:17,851 --> 00:10:21,700
That is its miss, minus twenty, the same one we squared earlier.

219
00:10:22,500 --> 00:10:26,153
One honest note. The real houses missed the line by up to twenty

220
00:10:26,153 --> 00:10:28,882
thousand. So treat two hundred thirty as a centre,

221
00:10:28,882 --> 00:10:31,420
give or take about twenty, not a promise.

222
00:10:32,220 --> 00:10:34,859
Eighteen hundred square feet sits inside our data,

223
00:10:34,859 --> 00:10:37,096
between houses we have actually seen.

224
00:10:37,096 --> 00:10:40,560
Guessing there is called prediction, or interpolation.

225
00:10:40,910 --> 00:10:43,615
Now try a mansion. Ten thousand square feet.

226
00:10:43,615 --> 00:10:46,780
The line says one point zero five million dollars.

227
00:10:47,130 --> 00:10:51,122
Maybe. But we have never seen a house that big on this street.

228
00:10:51,122 --> 00:10:53,880
We do not know if the straight line keeps going.

229
00:10:54,230 --> 00:10:56,310
Prices might level off, or jump.

230
00:10:56,310 --> 00:10:59,810
The line has no idea. Guessing outside your data is called

231
00:10:59,810 --> 00:11:02,300
extrapolation, and it is risky.

232
00:11:03,100 --> 00:11:04,961
It goes wrong the other way too.

233
00:11:04,961 --> 00:11:07,453
A two hundred square foot shed, by this line,

234
00:11:07,453 --> 00:11:09,520
would cost seventy thousand dollars.

235
00:11:09,870 --> 00:11:11,704
There is a deeper reason too.

236
00:11:11,704 --> 00:11:15,554
The line only ever saw five ordinary houses on one street.

237
00:11:15,554 --> 00:11:18,800
It cannot know anything the data never showed it.

238
00:11:19,400 --> 00:11:21,335
A line is a local summary.

239
00:11:21,335 --> 00:11:23,650
It is only trusted where the dots are.

240
00:11:24,650 --> 00:11:27,966
One more warning, and it is a big one.

241
00:11:27,966 --> 00:11:30,822
A line tells you that two things move together.

242
00:11:30,822 --> 00:11:32,480
It does not tell you why.

243
00:11:32,830 --> 00:11:36,410
Here, it feels obvious that more space makes a house cost more.

244
00:11:36,410 --> 00:11:38,200
But the line did not prove it.

245
00:11:38,200 --> 00:11:39,990
We knew it from common sense.

246
00:11:40,340 --> 00:11:43,729
Lines can link things that have nothing to do with each other.

247
00:11:43,729 --> 00:11:46,907
Ice cream sales and sunburns rise and fall together,

248
00:11:46,907 --> 00:11:49,240
with a lovely straight line between them.

249
00:11:49,590 --> 00:11:51,930
Ice cream does not cause sunburn.

250
00:11:51,930 --> 00:11:53,740
Hot sunny days cause both.

251
00:11:54,540 --> 00:11:56,612
The same thing can hide in house prices.

252
00:11:56,612 --> 00:12:00,551
Bigger houses are often newer, or in better neighbourhoods.

253
00:12:00,551 --> 00:12:03,844
Some of the hundred dollars per square foot may really belong to

254
00:12:03,844 --> 00:12:04,270
those.

255
00:12:04,620 --> 00:12:07,605
Separating those effects needs more than one input,

256
00:12:07,605 --> 00:12:10,590
and that is a later video on multiple regression.

257
00:12:11,190 --> 00:12:15,090
So read the slope as, houses that are bigger tend to cost this

258
00:12:15,090 --> 00:12:18,980
much more. Not, adding a room will raise the price by exactly

259
00:12:18,980 --> 00:12:19,430
this.

260
00:12:20,430 --> 00:12:25,010
Now let us see where this exact idea shows up in the real world.

261
00:12:25,360 --> 00:12:29,065
In machine learning, a linear regression is the first model you

262
00:12:29,065 --> 00:12:31,965
train. The five houses are the training data,

263
00:12:31,965 --> 00:12:33,620
and the line is the model.

264
00:12:33,970 --> 00:12:36,791
The sum of squared errors is called a loss.

265
00:12:36,791 --> 00:12:39,829
Training means finding the settings with the smallest loss,

266
00:12:39,829 --> 00:12:42,650
exactly like our line pivoting and sliding.

267
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:45,221
In data science, it is the trend line.

268
00:12:45,221 --> 00:12:48,189
How much do sales rise for each extra thousand dollars of

269
00:12:48,189 --> 00:12:50,410
advertising? The slope answers it.

270
00:12:50,760 --> 00:12:54,286
In finance, analysts plot a stock's daily moves against the

271
00:12:54,286 --> 00:12:57,411
whole market's moves. The slope of that line has a name,

272
00:12:57,411 --> 00:13:00,937
beta. A beta above one means the stock swings more than the

273
00:13:00,937 --> 00:13:01,570
market.

274
00:13:01,920 --> 00:13:06,136
In computer science, engineers time a program on bigger and

275
00:13:06,136 --> 00:13:08,354
bigger inputs and fit a line.

276
00:13:08,354 --> 00:13:12,321
The slope is the cost per item, and it tells them how long a

277
00:13:12,321 --> 00:13:13,540
huge job will take.

278
00:13:13,890 --> 00:13:16,607
But notice that last one is an extrapolation.

279
00:13:16,607 --> 00:13:19,900
Engineers check it, for the same reason we did with the mansion.

280
00:13:20,700 --> 00:13:23,753
Measuring how good a line is deserves its own video.

281
00:13:23,753 --> 00:13:26,618
For now, notice that our line cut the score from twenty six

282
00:13:26,618 --> 00:13:28,160
thousand to one thousand.

283
00:13:28,960 --> 00:13:31,130
Here are the mistakes beginners make most often.

284
00:13:31,480 --> 00:13:34,590
Mistake one. Forcing a straight line onto a curve.

285
00:13:34,940 --> 00:13:38,463
The formula will always give you a line, even when the dots

286
00:13:38,463 --> 00:13:40,142
bend. It never warns you.

287
00:13:40,142 --> 00:13:42,220
So always look at the picture first.

288
00:13:42,570 --> 00:13:46,282
Picture a scatter that rises steeply and then flattens out,

289
00:13:46,282 --> 00:13:48,830
like test scores against hours of study.

290
00:13:48,830 --> 00:13:52,076
A straight line cuts through the bend, too low in the middle and

291
00:13:52,076 --> 00:13:53,460
too high at both ends.

292
00:13:54,060 --> 00:13:57,502
If the misses are all positive in the middle and negative at the

293
00:13:57,502 --> 00:13:59,210
ends, the line is the wrong shape.

294
00:14:00,010 --> 00:14:03,961
Mistake two. Forgetting how much one odd point can pull the

295
00:14:03,961 --> 00:14:04,430
line.

296
00:14:04,780 --> 00:14:07,226
Say the three thousand square foot house had a gold-plated

297
00:14:07,226 --> 00:14:10,080
kitchen and sold for five hundred sixty thousand instead.

298
00:14:10,430 --> 00:14:14,014
Because every miss gets squared, that one house has a huge

299
00:14:14,014 --> 00:14:17,598
effect on the line. The slope jumps from one hundred to one

300
00:14:17,598 --> 00:14:19,710
hundred eighty dollars per square foot.

301
00:14:20,060 --> 00:14:22,610
And your fair price moves from two hundred thirty thousand to

302
00:14:22,610 --> 00:14:23,850
two hundred fifty four.

303
00:14:24,200 --> 00:14:27,557
It is the same reason one billionaire drags up an average

304
00:14:27,557 --> 00:14:30,170
income. Check odd points before you trust the slope.

305
00:14:30,970 --> 00:14:34,382
Mistake three. Trusting the line far outside the data,

306
00:14:34,382 --> 00:14:35,974
or reading it as cause.

307
00:14:35,974 --> 00:14:39,386
We just saw both. A line describes the dots you have,

308
00:14:39,386 --> 00:14:40,520
and nothing more.

309
00:14:41,320 --> 00:14:44,567
And a bonus one. Measuring the misses slanted,

310
00:14:44,567 --> 00:14:46,191
instead of straight up and down.

311
00:14:46,191 --> 00:14:49,820
The miss is always in the units of what you are predicting.

312
00:14:50,620 --> 00:14:52,490
Let us put the whole method on one card.

313
00:14:52,840 --> 00:14:56,460
One. Plot the dots, and check they look like a line.

314
00:14:56,810 --> 00:14:59,185
Two. Measure each miss straight up and down,

315
00:14:59,185 --> 00:15:00,910
in the units you predict.

316
00:15:01,260 --> 00:15:03,607
Three. Square the misses and add them.

317
00:15:03,607 --> 00:15:04,780
That is the score.

318
00:15:05,130 --> 00:15:07,519
Four. Find the line with the smallest score.

319
00:15:07,519 --> 00:15:11,203
Slope from the formula, then the intercept from the average

320
00:15:11,203 --> 00:15:11,640
point.

321
00:15:11,990 --> 00:15:15,333
Five. Read the slope as a rate, and predict only where you have

322
00:15:15,333 --> 00:15:15,940
data.

323
00:15:16,740 --> 00:15:18,496
A line does not know the truth.

324
00:15:18,496 --> 00:15:21,130
It is just the one guess that misses least.

325
00:15:22,630 --> 00:15:26,603
Look around your world, and notice how often one number seems to

326
00:15:26,603 --> 00:15:29,914
climb with another, and ask whether it is a line,

327
00:15:29,914 --> 00:15:32,120
a curve, or just a coincidence.

328
00:15:32,720 --> 00:15:34,736
If this lesson made things clear for you,

329
00:15:34,736 --> 00:15:37,551
please subscribe to Math Lab TV, so we can keep building

330
00:15:37,551 --> 00:15:38,370
together.

331
00:15:39,170 --> 00:15:40,590
But here's a puzzle for next time.

332
00:15:40,940 --> 00:15:44,479
With five houses and one straight line, we had a formula.

333
00:15:44,479 --> 00:15:47,384
But a modern AI model has millions of settings,

334
00:15:47,384 --> 00:15:49,050
and no formula at all.

335
00:15:49,400 --> 00:15:51,640
So how does it find its smallest score?

336
00:15:51,640 --> 00:15:53,206
It does what our line did.

337
00:15:53,206 --> 00:15:56,564
It feels which way is downhill, and takes a small step.

338
00:15:56,564 --> 00:15:58,130
That is gradient descent.

339
00:15:58,930 --> 00:16:00,510
I will see you in the next video.
